# DIRECT

DIRECT is a deterministic global optimizer for a scalar function over a
finite box. It divides the box into rectangles and samples their centers,
balancing further exploration of large regions against refinement around
good objective values. It needs neither derivatives nor a supplied
Lipschitz constant.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/direct/struct.Direct.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function on a finite box |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Deterministic global partitioning |
| Constraints | A box-only declaration through `Constraints` |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider DIRECT when the search region has meaningful finite bounds and
you want a reproducible exploration of several possible minima.
[Jones, Perttunen, and Stuckman (1993)](#ref-jones-perttunen-stuckman-1993)
introduced the method to balance local and global search without choosing
a Lipschitz constant. Its rectangle geometry is most practical when the
number of free coordinates is modest.

Compare [differential evolution](../de/) or
[CMA-ES](../cma-es/) when a stochastic population search suits the problem.
[GBNM](../gbnm/) instead combines bounded local simplex searches with
random restarts. If you already have a promising starting point and need
local refinement, consider [BOBYQA](../../derivative-free/bobyqa/).

A finite DIRECT run provides the best sampled point, rather than a
certificate that no lower point exists elsewhere in the box. In
particular, a small rectangle around the incumbent describes local search
resolution; other rectangles can remain large.

## How it works

DIRECT normalizes each free coordinate to `[0, 1]` and evaluates the center
of the resulting unit box. Coordinates with equal lower and upper bounds
are fixed and do not enter the partition geometry.

For each rectangle, the solver keeps the objective at its center and the
length of its Euclidean half-diagonal. A rectangle is potentially optimal
when its cost minus `K` times its radius is competitive for some positive
value of `K`, and its predicted improvement clears the configured margin.
Small values of `K` favor good observed costs; large values favor broad
unexplored rectangles. Basin selects across these trade-offs using a lower
convex hull.

The solver samples two new centers along each longest axis of a selected
rectangle, then trisects along those axes. Axes with better samples split
first, giving their promising samples larger child rectangles. One
iteration processes the entire selected set, from larger rectangles to
smaller ones. Exact cost ties are retained.

Basin implements original DIRECT, using Euclidean half-diagonals. The
locally biased DIRECT-L variant has different selection behavior and is
not the algorithm described here.

## Example

This two-dimensional quartic has several local minima on `[-5, 5]²`.
DIRECT searches the box until it finds an objective below `-78.33` or
reaches the evaluation budget. The `PointState` parameter supplies the
vector's shape; each fresh DIRECT run starts at the box midpoint,
regardless of the values in that vector.

<!-- compile: solver-direct -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Direct, Executor, PointState,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct BoundedQuartic {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl CostFunction for BoundedQuartic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter()
            .map(|v| 0.5 * (v.powi(4) - 16.0 * v * v + 5.0 * v))
            .sum())
    }
}

impl Constraints for BoundedQuartic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = BoundedQuartic {
        lower: vec![-5.0; 2],
        upper: vec![5.0; 2],
    };
    let solver = Direct::new();

    let result = Executor::new(problem, solver, PointState::new(vec![0.0; 2]))
        .target_objective(-78.33)
        .max_cost_evals(2_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < -78.33);
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| (-5.0..=5.0).contains(v)));
}
```

The objective target gives this example a useful stopping point. It does
not ask DIRECT to identify the exact minimizer or exhaust the box.

## Practical considerations

### Choose the box deliberately

The problem must declare `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>` with both bound
vectors present, finite, ordered, and matching the parameter dimension.
Additional constraint blocks are unsupported. Equal endpoints pin a
coordinate; an entirely fixed box needs just one evaluation.

Normalization gives each free interval the same initial geometric weight.
Very wide bounds therefore spend resolution over a large physical range,
even if the useful region occupies a small part of it. Use bounds that
describe the intended global search region.

### Separate selection from stopping

`with_epsilon` sets the potential-improvement margin and defaults to
`1e-4`. Smaller values permit more local refinement; zero removes the
required improvement margin. It controls rectangle selection, rather than
the accuracy of the reported solution.

`with_absolute_radius_tolerance` defaults to `Some(1e-6)` and measures the
best rectangle's half-diagonal after normalization. Optional
`with_relative_volume_tolerance` measures its volume relative to the
original box and is disabled by default. Enabled geometry checks combine
with OR. `None` disables a check, and zero requests exact zero. Volumes
shrink rapidly with dimension, so a small relative volume can coexist with
substantial uncertainty along individual coordinates.

### Budget for complete sweeps

Use executor limits such as `max_cost_evals`, `max_iter`, or `max_time`.
The executor checks limits between complete subdivision sweeps. A sweep
can evaluate many points, particularly when several rectangles tie, so the
last sweep can exceed an evaluation budget. Storage grows with both the
number of coordinates and the number of sampled rectangles.

Independent probes use `Problem::cost_batch`. The optional `parallel`
feature can evaluate them concurrently while preserving the numerical
results for a deterministic objective. Callback scheduling can change.

### Handle rejected evaluations consistently

`NaN` and positive infinity reject a sample. Basin additionally explores
the oldest largest rejected rectangle in each sweep, allowing recovery
when the initial midpoint is rejected. Geometry stopping requires a
usable incumbent; an all-rejected search needs an executor limit. Negative
infinity is accepted and stops the search. Application errors propagate.

If floating-point resolution prevents a split from producing distinct
probes in both normalized and original coordinates, DIRECT reports
`NumericalNoProgress`. Tighter geometric tolerances cannot overcome that
limit.

## Implementation

`PointState` publishes the best sampled center and its matching objective.
The solver owns the partition and all rectangle-selection work. Retain it
with `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `best_rectangle()`, including
the normalized center, radius, trisection depths, free-coordinate indices,
and rectangle count. This diagnostic performs no evaluations.

Fresh initialization clears the partition and evaluates the midpoint.
Reusing a previous point does not warm-start the partition. Exact
solver-and-state checkpoints preserve the partition and evaluation counts
and skip initialization. Keep the bounds and objective unchanged across
that continuation. The evolving solver is serializable with `serde`.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Custom
vectors need only `Clone`, `VectorLen`, and `VectorIndex<F>`; see the API's
[Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/direct/struct.Direct.html#backends).
The solver uses no matrix factorization or BLAS/LAPACK provider.

